.TH SMARTCOPY 1 "2026-11-24" "smartcopy v12.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
smartcopy \- resumable, self-checking copy of a drive or folder on macOS, with a plain-English report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo smartcopy
.RI [ options ]
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B sudo smartcopy \-\-verify\-only
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B sudo smartcopy \-\-dry\-run
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B sudo smartcopy \-\-speed\-test
.I SOURCE DESTINATION
.br
.B sudo smartcopy \-\-stop
.I DESTINATION
.br
.B sudo smartcopy \-\-help
.PP
If smartcopy isn't installed, run it from its folder as
.B sudo ./smartcopy.sh
with the same options.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B smartcopy
copies everything inside
.I SOURCE
into
.IR DESTINATION .
Either can be a whole drive (for example
.IR /Volumes/Archive )
or a folder on any drive. The destination folder is created if it doesn't exist.
.PP
It is built to finish no matter what happens along the way:
.IP \(bu 3
If it is interrupted (Ctrl\-C, a closed window, a crash, a power cut, an unplugged drive), run the
.B exact same command
again and it continues where it stopped.
.IP \(bu 3
If a drive disconnects, it waits for the drive to come back and carries on.
.IP \(bu 3
Failed files are retried automatically. Files that keep failing the same way (for example because of
damaged areas on a drive) are reported instead of being retried for ever.
.IP \(bu 3
At the end it checks every file's contents, re-copies any file that doesn't match, and checks it again.
.IP \(bu 3
It finishes with a report in plain English: what went wrong, on which drive, how to fix it and how to
avoid it next time.
.PP
It never deletes anything. Files already in the destination that aren't in the source are left alone;
files with the same name are replaced by the source's version.
.SH WHAT YOU SEE WHILE IT RUNS
The work is shown as numbered steps, each saying what it is doing and what comes next:
.PP
.RS
.nf
======== STEP 1 of 4: List the files ========
======== STEP 2 of 4: Copy the files ========
======== STEP 3 of 4: Final pass ========
======== STEP 4 of 4: Check every file ========
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.B \-\-no\-verify
there are 3 steps; with
.B \-\-verify\-only
there are 2 (list, check). During copying and checking, one status line updates every few seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Copying 52% \(md 1.2 TB of 2.3 TB \(md 14574 files left \(md 84 MB/s \(md TIME LEFT: 3h 40m \(md FINISHES \(~~ 15:20
.fi
.RE
.PP
The time left takes into account both the amount of data and the number of files left, and only
moves gradually. It appears after about a minute of progress (after about 5 minutes on big jobs,
which first measure the best speed setting). In a narrow Terminal window, less important parts are
dropped first; the time left and finish time are always shown.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation question on the first run. Later runs of the same copy never ask.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background
Keep running after Terminal is closed. Progress goes to a console log in the
.I rsync\-logs
folder; you get a notification when it finishes and the report opens in TextEdit.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show how many files and how much data would be copied, without copying anything.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Copy only; skip the final check of every file's contents. Run the check later with
.BR \-\-verify\-only .
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-only
Only check an earlier copy. Nothing is copied unless a file doesn't match, in which case just that
file is copied again. A check stopped part-way continues where it stopped on the next run.
.TP
.B \-\-stop
Stop a running copy cleanly (give only the destination). Run the same copy command again to resume.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Copy or check
.I N
files at the same time (1\-16). By default this is tuned automatically: big jobs try 1 and 4 at a time
for about two minutes each and keep whichever is faster.
.TP
.B \-\-speed\-test
Measure both drives for about a minute (about 600 MB of test files on the destination, deleted
afterwards) and explain what limits the speed and whether
.B \-\-jobs
helps.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Override the safety checks: source and destination apparently swapped, a Time Machine backup as the
source, or the whole startup disk as the source.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short summary.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. Use it before paths that start with a dash.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Everyday use
Copy a whole drive into a folder on another drive:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy just one folder:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy "/Volumes/Photos SSD/2024" "/Volumes/Backup/Photos/2024"
sudo smartcopy ~/Projects "/Volumes/Backup/Projects"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resume after any interruption: run the same command again.
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tip: type the start of the command, then drag the drive or folder from Finder into Terminal to paste
its exact path. Paths with spaces need quotes (or a backslash before each space).
.SS Previewing
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy \-\-dry\-run "/Volumes/Media Drive" "/Volumes/Backup/Media"
.fi
.RE
.SS Long copies
Start in the background, close Terminal, check progress, stop, resume:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy \-\-background /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
tail \-f "/Volumes/Backup/rsync\-logs/console\-20261124\-094854.log"
sudo smartcopy \-\-stop "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
sudo smartcopy /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.SS Copy now, check later
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy \-\-no\-verify /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
sudo smartcopy \-\-verify\-only /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.SS Speed
.RS
.nf
sudo smartcopy \-\-speed\-test /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
sudo smartcopy \-\-jobs 4 /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
sudo smartcopy \-\-jobs 1 /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.SS Unattended
No question on the first run, and a longer patience for a flaky connection:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo MAX_STUCK=12 smartcopy \-\-yes \-\-background /Volumes/Archive "/Volumes/Backup/Archive"
.fi
.RE
.SS Reading the private file list
.RS
.nf
open \-e "/Volumes/Backup/rsync\-logs/affected\-files\-20261124\-094854.txt"
.fi
.RE
.SS Removing old logs from the Mac (left by versions before v12; wait until any old copy finishes)
.RS
.nf
sudo rm \-rf ~/rsync\-logs
.fi
.RE
.SH HOW CHECKING WORKS
The check reads every file and compares fingerprints (xxh128 hashes) of the source and destination
copies. Each file's fingerprint is also saved in
.I .smartcopy\-hashes
on the destination, while copying and while checking. After that, files with a saved fingerprint are
checked by reading
.B only the destination
\(em usually much faster when the source is the slower drive \(em and a backup can be re-checked even
without the source connected. Files that changed on the source since they were copied are always
compared with the source instead.
.SH PRIVACY AND WHERE FILES ARE WRITTEN
File names are never shown on screen, in the report or in notifications. Where it matters which files
are affected, the report gives a count and points to a private file list.
.PP
Everything the script writes goes on the
.B destination
drive. Nothing is written to the Mac's own drive or to the source drive. If the destination drive isn't
connected, the script waits and creates nothing in its place.
.PP
On exFAT and FAT drives, files can't be restricted to one user: anyone with the drive can open the
logs, just as they can open the copied files. For real confidentiality, use an encrypted APFS
destination.
.SH FILES
On the destination drive, in the hidden folder
.I rsync\-logs
at the top of the drive (inside the destination folder if it isn't an external drive). Press
Cmd\-Shift\-. in Finder to see hidden items.
.TP
.I report\-STAMP.txt
The plain-English report for each run.
.TP
.I affected\-files\-STAMP.txt
Private list of the files behind each problem.
.TP
.I copy\-STAMP.log
Everything rsync did.
.TP
.I verify\-STAMP.txt
Results of the check (only differences are listed).
.TP
.I console\-STAMP.log
Screen output of a
.B \-\-background
run.
.TP
.I speedtest\-STAMP.txt
Results of
.BR \-\-speed\-test .
.TP
.I locks/
Stops two copies writing to the same destination at the same time.
.TP
.I .verified\-*
Which files a stopped check has already confirmed, so it can resume.
.PP
In the destination folder:
.TP
.I .smartcopy\-target
Records what the destination is a copy of; used to resume without asking and to catch swapped
source and destination.
.TP
.I .smartcopy\-hashes
Saved fingerprints of every file.
.TP
.I .rsync\-partial/
Half-copied files, kept aside until finished (APFS and Mac OS Extended destinations only; on exFAT,
files are written in place and an unfinished one is simply copied again).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Finished: copied and verified, checked, copied with
.BR \-\-no\-verify ,
dry run, or cancelled before starting.
.TP
.B 1
Stopped because of a problem the report explains (for example a full or read-only destination,
a missing source folder, or a setup error).
.TP
.B 2
Some files still differ from the source after repair.
.TP
.B 3
Copied, except files that couldn't be read or written; they are counted in the report and listed
privately.
.TP
.B 20
Stopped by you (Ctrl\-C or
.BR \-\-stop ).
Run the same command again to resume.
.PP
If it gives up after repeated passes with no progress, it exits with rsync's own error code.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Put these after
.B sudo
and before the script name.
.TP
.B MAX_STUCK
Give up after this many passes in a row that copy nothing new (default 6).
.TP
.B MAX_PASSES
Hard upper limit on passes (default 500).
.TP
.B JOBS
Same as
.BR \-\-jobs .
.SH INSTALLATION
From the smartcopy folder:
.PP
.RS
.nf
brew install rsync
sudo ./install.sh                 # install or update
sudo ./install.sh \-\-uninstall     # remove
.fi
.RE
.PP
This installs
.I /usr/local/bin/smartcopy
and
.IR /usr/local/share/man/man1/smartcopy.1 .
Updating while a copy is running is safe; don't start a second copy to the same destination until it
finishes. Copies made by older versions, including the old name safe\-copy, are picked up
automatically.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 3
macOS, run with
.BR sudo .
.IP \(bu 3
Homebrew (https://brew.sh) and rsync 3.2 or newer from it:
.BR "brew install rsync" .
Homebrew's rsync includes xxhash, used for the fast checksums. The rsync that comes with macOS is too old.
.IP \(bu 3
Terminal with Full Disk Access (System Settings > Privacy & Security > Full Disk Access), or
protected files can't be read.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 3
Keep a MacBook plugged in with the lid open; the script stops the Mac sleeping, but closing the lid
still sleeps it. If it does sleep, run the same command again.
.IP \(bu 3
exFAT destinations work but are slower on a Mac and don't keep owners, permissions, Finder tags or
symbolic links. For Mac-only drives, APFS is faster and keeps everything.
.IP \(bu 3
This makes a data copy, not a bootable clone of a macOS system.
.IP \(bu 3
File names containing a tab or a line break aren't supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsync (1),
.BR diskutil (8),
.BR caffeinate (8)
